//! English explanations of System › OS Log (G4.8b), scope `os_log`, sorted by key.

/// Explanations: (key, Markdown).
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("menu.os-log", "## What it does\nThe log of the **operating system** under the router: the kernel (NIC drivers, network stack, memory, disks), the services and the logins. Not the router's own **Log**, which shows what CoreRouter itself did (commands, DHCP, NTP).\n\nEach line has its RFC 5424 level, its source and its message; repeats become one row with a count, and messages the router knows bring **What it means** and a button to the window that fixes it. On Linux it reads `/dev/kmsg` and the systemd journal; on the production image (no systemd), the kernel alone."),
    ("os_log.details", "## Details\n\nThe chosen line in full: where it came from, the driver or unit that wrote it, the device, the priority, the boot it was written in, how often it repeated and between which times, and the whole message to copy. A known message adds **What it means** and buttons to act."),
    ("os_log.find", "## Find\n\nFilters the rows this window holds by message text, driver or unit name (`r8169`, `sshd`), device (`ether2`) or process id (PID). Case-insensitive. Esc clears it."),
    ("os_log.level", "## Level\n\nThe line's severity, in the eight levels of **RFC 5424** (§6.2.1): emergency (0) … debug (7). The chips group them in three: **error** (0–3), **warning** (4), **info** (5–7); the number is how many rows of that level the router keeps. No chip on: all."),
    ("os_log.meaning", "## What it means\n\nFor messages the router knows (NIC ring full, link flapping, conntrack full, OOM, disk error, machine check, thermal throttling, segfault, failed login…), what happened, the usual causes and what to do, with buttons to the right window. Those that cost traffic or hardware also go once to the router's **Log** (`system,error`)."),
    ("os_log.message", "## Message\n\nThe text the kernel or the program wrote, without control characters. A **37×** chip means the same message (but for its numbers) came 37 times; a blue chip means the router knows it — click the row to see what it means."),
    ("os_log.repeat", "## Repeated\n\nA message equal to one of the last 10 minutes — same source, unit, level and text but for its numbers — does not add a row: the row counts it, keeps the newest text and moves to the end. **repetido 37× em 9 min** says how often and between the first and the last."),
    ("os_log.source", "## Source\n\n- **kernel** — drivers, the network stack, memory, hardware; next to it, the driver.\n- **services** — programs and services (systemd units, daemons).\n- **auth** — logins and privileges: sshd, sudo, PAM (syslog facilities auth and authpriv).\n\nNo chip on: all."),
    ("os_log.sources", "## Sources\n\n- **kmsg** — `/dev/kmsg`, the kernel ring, first-hand; with `kernel.dmesg_restrict=1` it needs `CAP_SYSLOG` (red chip with the reason; the kernel lines then come from the journal).\n- **journal** — the systemd journal through `journalctl` (no shell, with a deadline). The production image has no systemd.\n- **lab** — the virtual lab's made-up lines (`--os-log host` reads the real machine)."),
    ("os_log.time", "## Time\n\nWhen the line was written, on the router's clock in UTC. A repeated row shows its **last** time; the details show the first and the last. Kernel times are since boot plus the boot time, like `dmesg -T`."),
];
